suite 166_phone_collection_suite.sql      # 0133 phone collection — Sean: "we should have the owner and any new person insert phone number on onboarding for safety and contact purposes". Most of the feature was ALREADY shipped (0049 _club_phone_visible decides who sees whose, phone_reveal audits it, the session shell renders a tel: chip, 0115:421 already nulls it in the tombstone); what was missing is that NOBODY COULD PUT A NUMBER IN — 0 of 10 profiles had one because `authenticated` has no UPDATE grant on the column (0091:198 names it OUT on purpose) and 127 W2 PINS that refusal. P1 normalises what humans type (hyphens · +82) rather than rejecting it · **P2 IS SEAN'S RULING** (§10 ④ 「Editable, but never blank」): empty/whitespace/punctuation each RAISE **and the previously stored number survives** — a raise that still wiped the column would satisfy "refuses" and defeat the ruling · P3 landline/short/long/alpha refused · P4 writes my row only (there is no target parameter to attack — the absence IS the party gate) · P5 tombstoned profile refused (0123 §5 posture) + anon cannot execute · **P6 THE POINT**: authenticated STILL has no direct UPDATE or SELECT on phone — a slice that shipped the function AND widened the grant would pass every pin above while giving away what the design protects · **P7 POSITIVE CONTROL** (140): the CHECK refuses hyphens at TABLE level (service_role bypasses the RPC entirely) AND still admits a valid number and NULL, because a constraint rejecting everything makes P3 green on a dead column. ⚠ Four shipped suites carried hyphenated fixtures and are normalised in the same slice (124 · 127 · 130 · 67) — the contract's §2 cost estimate named only two of them, and 67:18 built its value from md5() hex, which is not digits at all. ⚠ SHIP GATE UNMET: authored and landed, NOT live — nothing may collect a number until counsel's text exists (contract §8), and the client collection point is deliberately not wired. — P1~P7
suite 167_club_rsvp_hardening_suite.sql   # 0134 RSVP-family hardening — folds blind-review F4/F5/F6/F8. A1 delegated_only refuses a 동반 dog with **zero writes** (the gate sits above the people insert and this pin is what proves it) · A2 the dogless crew still get in (ruling #6) · A3/A4 mixed+owner_only re-pinned because §A recreates the function carrying them · A5 a stranger's dog answers not_your_dog, NOT companion_closed (order proof) · **B1 THE ARM'S REASON**: a delegated dog RSVP'd by an owner with no people row raises already_delegated AND LEAVES NO ORPHAN PEOPLE ROW — F4's real damage was the orphan, not the token (the old code inserted the people row, silently no-op'd the dog on session_dogs_active_uni, returned void, and the client fired haptic('success')) · B2 an ENDED delegation lets the companion row through and the history row survives · **B4 TOKEN HONESTY, and it CONSTRAINS THE IMPLEMENTATION**: a re-RSVP of one's own companion dog must answer already_joined, which forces `custody = 'runner_delegated'` on the pre-check — the contract's §B literally specifies the unfiltered predicate, and unfiltered it sits ABOVE the people insert and answers already_delegated instead (§B's shared-token justification is sound but describes belt 2, below the insert). Mutation M-B4 plants the unfiltered form and reddens B4, so the divergence is measured · C1 **F5**: the host puts their own dog in — owner_handled, responsible = host, zero bookings, people count unmoved · C2/C5 a dogless crew member can add a dog, and delegated_only refuses at the second door too · C3 not_joined · C4 not_your_dog · C7 the two-token split both ways (already_added vs already_delegated — safe here, unlike session_rsvp, because membership AND ownership are both already established) · C10 ACL · **D1 THE LOAD-BEARING ONE**: a checked-in participant cannot delete their record — people row, owner_handled dog row, participant_activities (the 0030:104 cascade) and session status all survive · D5 no_sh