suite 170_billing_key_swap_suite.sql      # 0137 the billing-key swap becomes ATOMIC — closes codex Critical #2 from the card-registration REJECT. The edge function checked `deleted_at`, AWAITED an external Toss call, then wrote; `delete_my_account_tx` could tombstone the profile inside that window and the already-authenticated request would then write a NEW key against it — a live standing authority to charge, on an account whose owner believes they are gone. A check-then-act across an external await cannot be fixed by ordering the statements more carefully; it has to stop being two, which is why the decision moved into the database where the row can be locked. B1 the ordinary swap · B2 replacing REPORTS the displaced key (codex #4 — the previous key stays live at the PG; the return value makes that visible instead of silent, and revocation is deliberately NOT done here because an outbound HTTP call inside the lock re-creates the defect) · **B3 THE CRITICAL**: a tombstoned profile is refused and NOTHING is written · B4 absent and tombstoned give the identical answer · **B5 THE SEAL**: service_role ONLY — `billing_keys` is RLS-on with zero policies so no client writes it, and a definer granted to `authenticated` reopens that through the back door · **B6 asserts the LOCK AS SOURCE**, because B1-B4 all stay green with the `for update` deleted — they run in one session where nothing competes for the row, so the lock is the one correctness argument no single-session test can exercise. ⚠ A true concurrent interleave needs two sessions and is not constructible in one plpgsql block; that limit is stated rather than hidden behind a green. — B1~B6
suite 171_billing_key_revocation_suite.sql # 0138 closes the last two codex REJECT findings. **#4** — replacing a card overwrote `billing_keys.billing_key` and the PREVIOUS key stayed LIVE at the PG with nothing recording it; 0137 made it visible, this makes it revocable. R1 a first link enqueues nothing (a row there would revoke the card just linked) · **R2** replacing enqueues the old key IN THE SAME TRANSACTION as the overwrite, so the obligation cannot be lost separately from the row that named it · R3 re-registering the SAME key enqueues nothing — a retry that reached Toss twice must not revoke the live card, which is why the predicate is `is distinct from` and not a bare `<>` · **R4** account deletion enqueues BEFORE it deletes, and the ordering is the whole property: the enqueue READS billing_keys, so placed after it finds nothing and the obligation vanishes silently — a failure indistinguishable from 「this account had no card」. The outbox FK is `on delete set null`, never cascade, because the orphan belonging to a DELETED profile is exactly the case that most needs revoking · R5 claim/report with `for update skip locked` so two ticks cannot claim one row · R6 a failure stays pending and retries until attempts exhaust, then `abandoned` — a key Toss will not delete is a fact to escalate, not a row to spin on · **R7** the registration gate is CLOSED BY DEFAULT (a NULL flag reads false; a future timestamp reads false), because defaulting a money-adjacent capability to ON because nobody set it is the 0116:425 fail-open in a different costume — codex #7, whose whole finding was that the protection lived only in a client constant and bound no modified client · R8 the seals: the outbox is RLS-on with ZERO policies (it holds live charging credentials awaiting revocation — more sensitive than the table it drains), every writer is service_role, and the gate READER is `authenticated` on purpose so the client can decline to draw a door the server would refuse. — R1~R8
suite 172_board_profile_ids_suite.sql     # 0139 the board carries profile ids so a row can route to a profile — Sean 2026-08-27 「for the tap for profile, yes make it like instagram」, which EXPLICITLY overrules 0136 contract R8 (「no runner_profile_id, no owner_profile_id … an id is a join key to every table above」). The reversal is recorded as a reversal: R8's reasoning was right for a board whose rows were not destinations, and Sean made them destinations. What an id actually discloses was re-measured rather than assumed — `profiles` already grants name/handle/avatar/district to any authenticated caller (0088:135) and `club_members` is `using (true)` (0030:131), so the id unlocks the SCREEN, not a new fact; the tables R8 named refuse an authenticated caller whatever id they hold. **I2 IS THE ONE REFUSAL THAT SURVIVED**: a pick-pending runner's id rides the IDENTICAL gate as their name, measured as a CONJUNCTION — an id disclosed where the name is hidden lets a third party read the name off the profile screen, i.e. the courtship leak arriving one hop later · I1 every named row carries its id · I3 acceptance publishes both · **I4 is a POSITIVE ALLOWLIST, not a forbidden-substring sweep** — this very migration proves a forbidden list can be legitimately amended, so the next amendment must be deliberate rather than slipping past a regex nobody re-read. — I1~I4
suite 173_owner_dog_limit_suite.sql   # 0140 Sean 「1 dog per person」 — the limit b6 measured as reader-less (owner_handled_dog_limit, 0 readers anywhere) becomes a BEFORE trigger on session_dogs, binding BOTH doors and any future writer at the one point all must pass. L1 second dog refused dog_limit through the real RPCs · L2 live-rows-only (an ended dog frees the slot) · L3 the custody-flip path nobody built yet is already bound (direct write, stated as such) · L4 raising the config to 2 admits a second dog — the trigger reads CONFIG, not a literal, then restores 1. — L1-L4
suite 174_revocation_lease_suite.sql      # 0141 corrects 0138 forward on four codex REJECT findings. **Every pin here exists because 0138's own battery was GREEN over the defect it names** — that is the useful fact: a green battery measured what it could reach. **L1** the deletion race no longer LOSES the key (0138 logged the orphan and threw; a log line is not a record and nothing reads it) — refused AND recorded AND not stored, all three, because any two without the third is a different bug · **L2 exclusivity, ASSERTED THIS TIME** — 0138 computed `second_claim` and never compared it to anything, a variable named after a property, holding the answer, worth nothing · **L3** an expired claim cannot overwrite a newer result (compare-and-set on the claim token; a late failure is discarded, not applied over a `done`) · **L4** a crashed worker's row returns on its own — 0138 stranded it forever at the attempt cap, invisible to both the claimer and the dispatcher's count, needing an operator nobody would tell · **L5** a queued key that becomes CURRENT again is abandoned rather than revoked, because the worker never re-reads `billing_keys` and would otherwise destroy a live card · **L6** the dispatcher reads the REAL vault contract — 0138 looked for `key` with a Bearer header while the authoritative secret (0116:349) is `{url, cron_key}` with `X-Cron-Key` and a BASE url, so it found nothing, returned 0, and scheduled revocation NEVER STARTED, indistinguishable from 「nothing due」. Asserted as SOURCE because a single-session suite cannot make an outbound call, and a green standing for an untested property is the thing this file is about. — L1~L6
